\import LambdaBox
-- Only `map`/`mkArray`/`Big`: importing Data.Array wholesale would shadow
-- String's `++` with the Array one, so array append is imported under the
-- alias `++A` (used in `appendStmts` and `compileClass` below).
\import Data.Array (map, mkArray, Big, ++ \as \infixr 5 ++A)
\import Data.Bool
\import Data.Maybe
\import Data.String
\import JavaAst
\import JavaPrint
\import Set
\import StringUtil

-- =============================================================================
-- lambda-box (LBTerm) -> Java generator.
--
-- It builds `JavaAst` values (`JavaAst.ard`), NOT source text: the target
-- language is defined by that AST, and spelling it out is the sole job of the
-- trusted printer (`JavaPrint.ard`). `compileProgram` composes the two, so
-- callers still get a `String`.
--
-- Termination approach: Arend's foetus/size-change checker rejects a separate
-- list-typed helper that calls compileExpr (it crosses the LBTerm <-> Array
-- LBTerm type boundary). So, following arend-lib's HRS.ard
-- (`\lam i => apply (arguments i)`), compileExpr recurses over Array children
-- INLINE via `xs i` / direct projections like `(brs i).2` (same type, indexed
-- sub-term / direct projection of the recursion argument) -- never through a
-- `\case`-bound variable, which loses the structural link the checker needs.
-- For the same reason `Data.Array.map` cannot be used HERE: it hides `args i`
-- behind a closure (verified: the checker rejects it), so the inline arrays are
-- built with `mkArray {..} {xs.len} (\lam i => .. (xs i))`.
-- The per-child results are then combined by array operations (`Data.Array`'s
-- `++`/`Big`/`map`) that do NOT call compileExpr, so they terminate on their
-- own; `map` is fine there and everywhere outside the recursion.
--
-- No fresh-name counter: a structural `path : String` is passed DOWN and
-- branched per child, so names are unique without left-to-right state
-- threading (which would force a sequential helper).
--
-- Design: untyped -> Java `Object`; closures = anonymous inner classes (no
-- invokedynamic); constructors -> `Data{int tag; Object[] fields}`; constants ->
-- zero-arg static methods; fix -> a local class with one method per mutually
-- defined function. The program's term becomes `body()`; no entry point is
-- generated (see `compileBody`).
--
-- The runtime (`Fn`, `Data`, `BOX`, `Rt.PRIM_*`) is NOT generated: it lives in
-- the hand-written `runtime/Rt.java` shipped alongside the output. Its CORE is
-- reached through the AST's runtime nodes (`tFn`, `jApply`, `jDataNew`, ... --
-- see JavaAst.ard's RUNTIME ABI), its periphery by qualified name through `rt`.
--
-- ONE TARGET, NO OPTIONS. This generator has no configuration: `compileProgram`
-- is a function of the program alone. There used to be a `JavaTarget` record
-- carrying the runtime class name, a `fix` encoding and an int representation,
-- with three instances. All three axes are now settled, and each was retired
-- for its own reason:
--
--   * the runtime class name had one value in every instance, and is now a
--     constant next to the AST nodes whose spelling it decides
--     (`runtimeClassName`, JavaAst.ard);
--   * the `fix` encoding was a genuine A/B (mutable `Fn[]` knot-tying vs the
--     local class), and it was decided -- see the `fix` clause of `compileExpr`
--     for what the surviving encoding buys;
--   * the int representation was `Long` or `BigInteger`, and `BigInteger` was
--     never the better approximation of λ□'s int63 (see KNOWN MISMATCH below),
--     only the unbounded one.
--
-- The point of removing them is not tidiness. Every option is a case a Java
-- semantics would have to interpret and a correctness proof would have to
-- discharge, so the configuration surface is part of the thing being verified.
-- A new representation (`int63`, the one that could actually be proved right)
-- should come back as a parameter when it is written, not be kept warm.
-- =============================================================================

-- Representation of λ□ primitive ints in the generated Java: `java.lang.Long`,
-- Java's built-in 64-bit integer.
--
-- KNOWN MISMATCH (documented, not fixed). A λ□ `prim (primInt, _)` is a 63-bit
-- machine integer with cyclic (mod 2^63) arithmetic -- MetaRocq's
-- erasure/theories/EPrimitive.v has `primIntModel (i : PrimInt63.int)`,
-- lean-to-lambdabox has `PrimModel .primInt := BitVec 63`, and the Rocq refman
-- ("Primitive objects / Primitive integers") specifies the width plus the
-- unsigned/signed views `Uint63`/`Sint63`. No Java type matches that, and our
-- own AST does not either (LambdaBox.ard's `PrimModel primInt => Int` is
-- unbounded). `Long` is an APPROXIMATION, and the closest one available: a
-- fixed-width integer with wraparound, so only the width is wrong (2^64 instead
-- of 2^63) and the sign convention differs, whereas `PrimInt63` reduction is
-- unsigned/cyclic.
--
-- It agrees with the C backend (CertiRocq's prim_int63_*) and the OCaml one
-- (unboxed OCaml `int`, cf. ExtrOCamlInt63) only while values stay well below
-- 2^62, which holds for the current examples.
--
-- `java.math.BigInteger` used to be selectable here and was REMOVED, not lost:
-- unbounded arithmetic matches our AST's `Int` but neither the source's int63
-- nor the other two backends, so it was further from the source semantics than
-- `Long` rather than more conservative -- and nothing in the pipeline selected
-- it.
-- FUTURE WORK: an int63 representation whose literals and `Rt.PRIM_*` ops
-- normalize mod 2^63 (following `Uint63`) -- the only one that could be proved
-- to agree with λ□'s evaluation relation once that relation exists. That is a
-- new REPRESENTATION, so it belongs here as a parameter when written; see
-- LambdaBox.ard's `PrimModel` and notes/RESEARCH_AND_PLAN.md.
\func intLit (i : Int) : JExpr => jCallStatic "Long.valueOf" (jLongLit i :: nil)

-- Suffix of the runtime's primitive-op constants (`Rt.PRIM_ADD_LONG`). Kept as
-- a name of its own rather than spelled into the axiom table below, because
-- `Rt.java` still ships a second family (`_INT`, on BigInteger) and an int63
-- family would be a third: this is the one place that says which one generated
-- code reaches for.
\func primOpSuffix : String => "LONG"

-- The runtime's PERIPHERY -- the primitive-op constants, the ill-formed-input
-- reporters, the entry point -- is referenced by qualified name: those are axiom
-- realizations and I/O, opaque to a λ□ semantics, so they get no AST node (see
-- the SCOPE paragraph in JavaAst.ard). Its CORE is `tFn`/`tData`/`jApply`/... in
-- the AST instead. `runtimeQual`/`runtimeClassName` come from JavaAst.ard: the
-- runtime's name is fixed, since it names the one semantic substrate every
-- generated program shares rather than a representation choice.
\func rt : String => runtimeQual

\func rtRef : JExpr => jStatic runtimeClassName

-- Realization of λ□ axioms (constants without a body) in the Java runtime.
-- Peregrine does this per run via `--attributes` (see ExampleMatMul.ard's
-- matMulAttrsC/OCaml, remapping onto CertiRocq's prim_int63_*); the Java
-- backend instead has this fixed table of axiom name -> runtime expression,
-- since the runtime it targets (`runtime/Rt.java`) is ours. An axiom listed
-- here compiles to the runtime constant, everything else to a throwing stub.
-- Keys are fully mangled kernames: the Lean frontend places machine-Nat
-- primitives under `Nat`, while the hand-written examples use root names.
-- Keeping the module path avoids treating an unrelated `Foo.add` as Nat.add.
\func javaAxioms : Array (\Sigma String JExpr) =>
  \let | op (nm : String) => jField rtRef ("PRIM_" ++ nm ++ "_" ++ primOpSuffix)
       -- An operation that neither takes nor returns a λ□ primitive int is the
       -- same in both representations, so it has ONE runtime constant.
       | fixed (nm : String) => jField rtRef nm
  \in ("c___prim_add_int", op "ADD") ::
      ("c___prim_mul_int", op "MUL") ::
      ("c___prim_sub_int", op "SUB") ::
      ("c___prim_eqb_int", op "EQB") ::
      ("c__Nat__add", op "ADD") ::
      ("c__Nat__mul", op "MUL") ::
      -- NOT `op "SUB"`: Lean's Nat subtraction truncates at zero, while
      -- `prim_sub_int` is Peregrine's wrapping int63 subtraction.
      ("c__Nat__sub", jField rtRef ("NAT_SUB_" ++ primOpSuffix)) ::
      ("c__Nat__beq", op "EQB") ::
      -- The rest of the machine-Nat operations lean-to-lambdabox leaves as
      -- axioms. `div`/`mod` follow Lean's total conventions and
      -- `decEq`/`decLe`/`decLt` return `Decidable`, not `Bool` (see Rt.java).
      ("c__Nat__div", op "DIV") ::
      ("c__Nat__mod", op "MOD") ::
      ("c__Nat__pow", op "POW") ::
      ("c__Nat__ble", op "BLE") ::
      ("c__Nat__blt", op "BLT") ::
      ("c__Nat__decEq", op "DEC_EQ") ::
      ("c__Nat__decLe", op "DEC_LE") ::
      ("c__Nat__decLt", op "DEC_LT") ::
      -- Lean's machine-`Int` operations. `Int` erases to the same λ□ primitive
      -- int as `Nat`, so `ofNat` is the identity in both representations.
      ("c__Int__ofNat", fixed "INT_OF_NAT") ::
      ("c__Int__negSucc", jField rtRef ("INT_NEG_SUCC_" ++ primOpSuffix)) ::
      ("c__Int__neg", jField rtRef ("INT_NEG_" ++ primOpSuffix)) ::
      ("c__Int__add", jField rtRef ("INT_ADD_" ++ primOpSuffix)) ::
      ("c__Int__mul", jField rtRef ("INT_MUL_" ++ primOpSuffix)) ::
      ("c__Int__ediv", jField rtRef ("INT_EDIV_" ++ primOpSuffix)) ::
      ("c__Int__emod", jField rtRef ("INT_EMOD_" ++ primOpSuffix)) ::
      ("c__Int__decEq", jField rtRef ("INT_DEC_EQ_" ++ primOpSuffix)) ::
      -- Lean's `Array` operations (`@[extern]`, so erasure keeps no body). Only
      -- `size` returns a Nat, hence only it depends on the representation. The
      -- Lean frontend writes `!` in a name as `_u33`.
      ("c__Array__mk", fixed "ARRAY_MK") ::
      ("c__Array__emptyWithCapacity", fixed "ARRAY_EMPTY_WITH_CAPACITY") ::
      ("c__Array__push", fixed "ARRAY_PUSH") ::
      ("c__Array__getInternal", fixed "ARRAY_GET_INTERNAL") ::
      ("c__Array__get_u33Internal", fixed "ARRAY_GET_BANG_INTERNAL") ::
      ("c__Array__set_u33", fixed "ARRAY_SET_BANG") ::
      ("c__Array__swap", fixed "ARRAY_SWAP") ::
      ("c__Array__size", jField rtRef ("ARRAY_SIZE_" ++ primOpSuffix)) ::
      -- Rewriting along an equality is the identity at runtime.
      ("c__Eq__rec", fixed "EQ_REC") ::
      ("c__Eq__ndrec", fixed "EQ_REC") ::
      nil

-- Lookup by fully mangled kername, including its module path.
\func lookupAxiom (name : String) (tbl : Array (\Sigma String JExpr)) : Maybe JExpr \elim tbl
  | nil => nothing
  | e :: tbl => \case e.1 == name \with {
    | true => just e.2
    | false => lookupAxiom name tbl
  }

-- Compilation result: (statements to run first, value expression).
\func Out : \Type => \Sigma (Array JStmt) JExpr

-- The environment maps a de Bruijn index to the EXPRESSION that denotes it:
-- usually a variable (`jVar "p3"`), but a `fix` slot is an array element
-- (`x3[0]`), which is why this is an `Array JExpr` and not an `Array JIdent`.
-- Head = index 0.
-- An index larger than the number of enclosing binders is ill-formed input (a
-- closed λ□ program has none), so instead of a silent `null` we emit a runtime
-- call that fails immediately and reports the index and the node.  `n0` is the
-- original index, kept for the message while `n` counts down.
\func nthVar (n : Nat) (env : Array JExpr) (n0 : Nat) (path : String) : JExpr \elim n, env
  | _, nil => jCallStatic (rt ++ "unbound") (jIntLit n0 :: jStringLit path :: nil)
  | 0, x :: _ => x
  | suc n, _ :: env => nthVar n env n0 path

-- Array append and flattening of per-child results. Both come from Data.Array;
-- neither calls compileExpr, so the recursion above stays in its own SCC.
-- `appendStmts` is the main place the aliased array append `++A` is used (see
-- the import).
\func appendStmts (xs ys : Array JStmt) : Array JStmt => xs ++A ys

\func concatStmtBlocks (bs : Array (Array JStmt)) : Array JStmt => Big appendStmts nil bs

-- The statements of a sequence of child results, in order.
\func concatStmts (rs : Array Out) : Array JStmt => concatStmtBlocks (map (\lam r => r.1) rs)

-- Kername -> Java method name. Every source component is encoded at this one
-- boundary; the fixed separators and leading `c_` are legal by construction.
\func joinDirs (ds : Array String) : String \elim ds
  | nil => ""
  | d :: nil => javaIdentPart d
  | d :: ds => javaIdentPart d ++ "_" ++ joinDirs ds

\func mangleModPath (mp : ModPath) : String \elim mp
  | mpFile dp => joinDirs dp
  | mpBound dp id n => joinDirs dp ++ "_" ++ javaIdentPart id ++ "_" ++ natToStr n
  | mpDot mp id => mangleModPath mp ++ "_" ++ javaIdentPart id

\func mangleKername (k : Kername) : String =>
  "c_" ++ mangleModPath k.kerModPath ++ "__" ++ javaIdentPart k.kerName

-- Bind a branch's fields to fresh locals, starting at field index `fieldIx`
-- (= npars, to skip inductive parameters). Returns (bind-statements, extended env).
-- Recurses on the binder array only; never calls compileExpr.
-- Pushing left-to-right makes the LAST binder de Bruijn index 0 (matches iota_red).
\func bindFields (dv : JExpr) (fieldIx : Nat) (bs : Array BinderName) (env : Array JExpr) (path : String)
  : \Sigma (Array JStmt) (Array JExpr) \elim bs
  | nil => (nil, env)
  | _ :: bs =>
    \let | nm => "g" ++ natToStr fieldIx ++ path
         | st => jLocal true tObject nm (just (jDataField dv fieldIx))
         | r  => bindFields dv (suc fieldIx) bs (jVar nm :: env) path
    \in (st :: r.1, r.2)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The local-class `fix` encoding; see the `fix` clause of `compileExpr`.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- The local class of the fix at `path`, and its j-th method. Both are derived
-- from the path, so they are unique without a counter, like every other
-- generated name here. `Fix` is capitalized because it names a class.
\func fixClassName (path : String) : String => "Fix" ++ path

\func fixMethodName (j : Nat) : String => "f" ++ natToStr j

-- The j-th mutually defined function AS A VALUE: a closure forwarding to the
-- method. λ□ lets a sibling be passed around, not only called, so the
-- environment has to hold an `Rt.Fn` -- and the method itself is not one.
--
-- Created at the POINT OF USE (one `new` per occurrence), deliberately:
--   * a wrapper cached in a field assigned by the constructor would put the
--     initialization order this encoding exists to remove straight back in;
--   * a wrapper bound to a local at the top of each method would allocate k of
--     them on every call, including the calls that never mention a sibling;
--   * per use, the overwhelmingly common occurrence -- a self-recursive call --
--     is `new`-then-`apply` with the closure not escaping, which is the shape
--     escape analysis is expected to remove entirely.
-- `recv` is the receiver to forward to: `FixP.this` from inside the class
-- (where `this` is the wrapper closure, not the fix), the instance local from
-- outside it.
\func fixMethodRef (recv : JExpr) (path : String) (j : Nat) : JExpr =>
  \let wp => "w" ++ natToStr j ++ path
  \in jClosure wp (jReturn (jInvoke recv (fixMethodName j) (jVar wp :: nil)) :: nil)

-- Push the k siblings onto env as forwarding closures; head = f(k-1) = de
-- Bruijn 0, the same order `pushFix` establishes for the array encoding
-- (λ□'s fix_subst). No compileExpr.
\func pushFixMethods (recv : JExpr) (path : String) (j k : Nat) (env : Array JExpr)
  : Array JExpr \elim k
  | 0 => env
  | suc k => pushFixMethods recv path (suc j) k (fixMethodRef recv path j :: env)

-- Core compiler. `path` uniquely identifies this node; binder names and child
-- paths are derived from it, so no state threading is needed.
\func compileExpr (env : Array JExpr) (path : String) (t : LBTerm) : Out \elim t
  | box => (nil, jBox)

  | bvar n => (nil, nthVar n env n path)

  -- A locally-nameless free variable has no Java counterpart at all (the
  -- serializer likewise emits `tVar_UNREACHABLE`), so compile it to a throwing
  -- runtime call naming the variable rather than to a silent `null`.
  | fvar v => (nil, jCallStatic (rt ++ "freeVar") (jStringLit v.varName :: jStringLit path :: nil))

  | prim (primInt, i) => (nil, intLit i)

  | const k => (nil, jCallStatic (mangleKername k) nil)

  | app f a =>
    \let | of => compileExpr env ("F" ++ path) f
         | oa => compileExpr env ("A" ++ path) a
    \in (appendStmts of.1 oa.1, jApply of.2 oa.2)

  | lambda _ body =>
    \let | pv => "p" ++ path
         | ob => compileExpr (jVar pv :: env) ("L" ++ path) body
    \in (nil, jClosure pv (appendStmts ob.1 (jReturn ob.2 :: nil)))

  | letIn _ value body =>
    \let | ov => compileExpr env ("V" ++ path) value
         | lv => "l" ++ path
         | ob => compileExpr (jVar lv :: env) ("B" ++ path) body
    \in (appendStmts ov.1 (jLocal true tObject lv (just ov.2) :: ob.1), ob.2)

  -- construct: recurse inline over the Array of args.
  | construct _ cidx args =>
    \let | results => mkArray {Out} {args.len} (\lam i => compileExpr env ("c" ++ natToStr i ++ path) (args i))
    \in (concatStmts results, jDataNew cidx (map (\lam r => r.2) results))

  -- case: switch on the scrutinee's tag; each branch binds fields[npars ..] then
  -- evaluates its body. We map inline over `brs i`, binding the body by pattern.
  -- The switch has no value, so the branches assign to a result local `r<path>`,
  -- which is this node's value expression.
  | case _ npars scrut brs =>
    \let | os => compileExpr env ("S" ++ path) scrut
         | dv => "d" ++ path
         | rv => "r" ++ path
         -- Recurse on the DIRECT projection `(brs i).2`, NOT a `\case`-bound
         -- variable: the checker tracks `(brs i).2 < t` (index into the sub-term
         -- `brs`, then project) but loses that through a `\case` pattern binding.
         | cases => mkArray {\Sigma Nat (Array JStmt)} {brs.len} (\lam i =>
             \let | bp  => "b" ++ natToStr i ++ path
                  | bnd => bindFields (jVar dv) npars (brs i).1 env bp
                  | ob  => compileExpr bnd.2 bp (brs i).2
             \in (i, appendStmts bnd.1 (appendStmts ob.1 (jAssign (jVar rv) ob.2 :: nil))))
    \in (appendStmts os.1
           (jLocal true tData dv (just (jCast tData os.2)) ::
            jLocal false tObject rv nothing ::
            jSwitch (jDataTag (jVar dv)) cases
              (jThrow (jNew (tClass "RuntimeException") (jStringLit "no matching branch" :: nil)) :: nil) ::
            nil),
         jVar rv)

  -- proj: the AST has no arithmetic node, so `npars + idx` is added here.
  | proj pinfo scrut =>
    \let | os => compileExpr env ("P" ++ path) scrut
    \in (os.1,
         jDataField (jCast tData os.2) (pinfo.projParamCount Nat.+ pinfo.projFieldIdx))

  -- fix: a LOCAL CLASS with one method per mutually defined function, the knot
  -- tied statically by the class declaration. `Fix<path>.this` is how a method
  -- reaches its siblings, since the forwarding closure's own `this` is the
  -- closure. Maps inline over `defs j`, projecting the body directly
  -- (`(defs j).defBody`), same reasoning as `case` above -- which is also what
  -- keeps the mutual recursion with `compileFixMethod` below within the
  -- termination checker's reach.
  --
  -- WHAT THIS ENCODING BUYS, since the alternative it replaced is gone and the
  -- reason should not go with it. The original was a mutable knot:
  -- `final Rt.Fn[] x = new Rt.Fn[k];` then `x[j] = ..` for each j, with the
  -- environment reading `x[j]` back. Its correctness rested on an invariant
  -- nothing stated or checked -- `x[j]` must not be DEREFERENCED before all k
  -- assignments have run. That holds for the λ□ terms erasure produces, because
  -- a fix body is a guarded lambda, so the read happens when the closure is
  -- applied; an unguarded body silently read `null`. Here there is no cell, no
  -- assignment, and nothing whose value depends on when it is read, so the side
  -- condition does not need stating -- and `compileFixMethod`'s second clause
  -- makes the unguarded case diverge loudly instead of reading an empty slot.
  -- (The two were also measured against each other: the local class cost 1-5%
  -- of run time, taken for the semantics. See test/benchmarks.md.)
  | fix defs idx =>
    \let | k     => defs.len
         | cls   => fixClassName path
         | inst  => "z" ++ path
         | env'  => pushFixMethods (jQualThis cls) path 0 k env
         | methods => mkArray {\Sigma JIdent JIdent (Array JStmt)} {k} (\lam j =>
             \let | m => compileFixMethod env' ("y" ++ natToStr j ++ path) (defs j).defBody
             \in (fixMethodName j, m.1, m.2))
    \in (jLocalClass cls methods ::
         jLocal true (tClass cls) inst (just (jNew (tClass cls) nil)) ::
         nil,
         fixMethodRef (jVar inst) path idx)

-- One method of a `fix` local class: (parameter name, body).
--
-- Mutually recursive with `compileExpr`, and it \elim's the fix def's body in
-- order to STRIP THE GUARDING LAMBDA -- an inner `\case` would not do, because
-- the termination checker loses the structural link through a `\case`-bound
-- variable (the same reason `case`/`fix` above recurse on direct projections).
-- Going through a separate `\elim` keeps the link: `compileExpr` calls this on
-- `(defs j).defBody < fix defs idx`, and this calls `compileExpr` on the
-- lambda's body, so the cycle decreases.
--
-- Stripping the lambda is the whole point: the guarded body becomes the METHOD's
-- body, so a self-recursive call is a call, not a closure allocation followed by
-- an interface dispatch.
--
-- The second clause is the general case, and it is why this encoding needs no
-- well-formedness side condition. An UNGUARDED body (`fix [f => f] 0`, or
-- anything else that is not syntactically a lambda) is eta-expanded: the method
-- evaluates the body and applies the result. Such a λ□ term diverges, and so
-- does this Java -- loudly, with a StackOverflowError, where the array encoding
-- silently dereferences a `null` slot. Erasure output is a lambda in practice,
-- so the clause is expected to be dead code; it emits a comment saying so, and
-- `grep` over generated programs is how that is confirmed.
\func compileFixMethod (env : Array JExpr) (path : String) (t : LBTerm)
  : \Sigma JIdent (Array JStmt) \elim t
  | lambda _ inner =>
    \let | pv => "p" ++ path
         | ob => compileExpr (jVar pv :: env) ("L" ++ path) inner
    \in (pv, appendStmts ob.1 (jReturn ob.2 :: nil))
  | t =>
    \let | pv => "p" ++ path
         | ot => compileExpr env path t
    \in (pv, jComment "unguarded fix body: eta-expanded (diverges instead of reading an unassigned slot)" ::
             appendStmts ot.1 (jReturn (jApply ot.2 (jVar pv)) :: nil))


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Program assembly.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Every generated member is a `public static Object f(){ .. }`, and `JMethod`
-- holds nothing else (see JavaAst.ard), so this is the whole of method assembly.
\func constMethod (name : String) (body : Array JStmt) : JMember =>
  jMethod (\new JMethod name body)

\func compileDecl (kd : \Sigma Kername GlobalDecl) : JMember =>
  \let | name => mangleKername kd.1
  \in \case kd.2 \with {
    | constantDecl cb => \case cb.cstBody \with {
      | just body =>
        \let | o => compileExpr nil "" body
        \in constMethod name (appendStmts o.1 (jReturn o.2 :: nil))
      | nothing => \case lookupAxiom name javaAxioms \with {
        | just impl => constMethod name (jReturn impl :: nil)
        | nothing =>
          constMethod name
            (jThrow (jNew (tClass "RuntimeException")
                          (jStringLit ("axiom " ++ name ++ " not implemented") :: nil)) :: nil)
      }
    }
    | inductiveDecl _ =>
      jMemberComment ("inductive " ++ name ++ ": erased; values use Data(tag, fields)")
  }

\func compileDecls (ds : GlobalDeclarations) : Array JMember =>
  map (\lam d => compileDecl d) ds

-- The NAME OF THE ENTRY VALUE. `body` is a fixed, unmangled name, and it cannot
-- collide with a compiled λ□ constant: `mangleKername` prefixes every one of
-- those with `c_`, so the whole unprefixed namespace is free.
\func bodyMethodName : JIdent => "body"

-- The program's term, as the one method a caller is expected to start from:
--
--   public static Object body(){ .. return <the program's value>; }
--
-- NO ENTRY POINT IS GENERATED. This used to emit `__main` plus a
-- `public static void main(String[])` that handed `__main` to `Rt.runMain`,
-- which made every output a runnable program -- and exactly for that reason not
-- a reusable one: two extracted classes could not sit in one program, because a
-- λ□ term is a VALUE, not a process, and deciding to print it (or to run it on a
-- thread with a particular stack size, or to feed it to something else) is the
-- caller's decision, not the extractor's.
--
-- So a `main` is now written by hand, outside. It is five lines, and
-- `runtime/Main.java.in` is the worked example the harness itself uses:
--
--   Rt.runMain(new Rt.Fn(){ public Object apply(Object ignored){
--     return <Class>.body();
--   } });
--
-- `Rt.runMain` is unchanged and still where the stack reasoning lives (λ□ `fix`
-- becomes non-tail Java recursion, and the primordial thread's stack is set by
-- the OS, not by `-Xss` -- see runtime/Rt.java). Moving `main` out moved that
-- decision to the caller; it did not delete it.
\func compileBody (t : LBTerm) : Array JMember =>
  \let | o => compileExpr nil "" t
  \in constMethod bodyMethodName (appendStmts o.1 (jReturn o.2 :: nil)) :: nil

-- Only the program-specific class is generated; `Fn`, `Data` (with its
-- human-readable `toString`) and `BOX` come from the hand-written runtime class
-- `runtime/Rt.java`, which must be compiled together with this output.
--
-- `name` is what to call the class. It is an ARGUMENT and not the constant
-- `Prog` it used to be: two λ□ programs extracted independently have to be able
-- to coexist in one Java program, which a fixed name makes impossible. It is not
-- a `JavaTarget`-style option either -- it says what to call the output, not how
-- to compile it, and the same term may legitimately be emitted under two names.
--
-- NOTE (not done here): coexisting is not yet the same as COOPERATING. A λ□
-- `const` compiles to an unqualified `c_foo()` -- see `compileExpr` -- so it is
-- resolved inside the enclosing class, and two classes still cannot call each
-- other's constants nor share one copy of `Nat.add`. That needs a resolver
-- `Kername -> Maybe JQIdent` threaded into `compileExpr`, which is the natural
-- generalization of `javaAxioms` (an axiom IS a kername resolved to an external
-- name). Naming the class is the prerequisite, not the feature.
\func compileClass (name : JIdent) (p : LBProgram) : JClass =>
  \new JClass name (compileDecls p.1 ++A compileBody p.2)

-- The generator and the printer, composed: the whole backend, as a function of
-- the class name and the program, and nothing else. There is no
-- `compileProgramWith` any more -- there is no configuration left to pass (see
-- ONE TARGET, NO OPTIONS at the top).
\func compileProgram (name : JIdent) (p : LBProgram) : String =>
  printClass (compileClass name p)

-- Print a generated program with `putStrLn` (Data.String -> console).
-- \import Debug
-- \func main => putStrLn (compileProgram "Prog" (nil, prim (primInt, -44)))